        "description": "<p class=\"is-style-drop-cap-with-border wp-block-paragraph\">It may take a while to become as famous as Yosemite, the Grand Canyon, or Monument Valley, but the 12,000-acre Chiricahua area in southeast Arizona just became the 64th national park in the U.S.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Known as \u201cthe wonderland of rocks,\u201d Chiricahua was shaped by ancient volcanic forces and contains a wealth of dramatic geological features. It also boasts a rich human history, including tales of the Chiricahua Apache who lived here long before colonization from the Spanish, the African-American soldiers who were stationed here in the 1880s, and homesteading families who moved here from the eastern U.S. in the late 1800s.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One highlight: <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/chir/learn/historyculture/massai-point.htm\">Massai Point</a>. Named after a Chiricahua Apache man born in the Mescal Mountains, the landmark features spires and columns carved into rhyolite rock (originally",
